            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the angle of each complex number: the direction from the origin to the point a + b j, measured counter-clockwise from the positive real axis. It is the phase of a frequency in a spectrum - where in its cycle that wave starts.\n\nTogether with abs (the length, the magnitude) it is the polar form of a complex number, as atan2 and hypot are for a point (x, y). Real numbers have angle 0, or 180 degrees (pi) when negative.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.angle\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA number, list or array, complex or not. Every input is converted and sent.\n\ndegrees:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default) gives radians, -pi to pi, as NumPy does;\non gives degrees, -180 to 180. The demo has it on.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nangle:\nThe angle of each number, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe spectrum of 0 1 0 0 - a single pulse one step in - is 1, -1j, -1 and 1j: four complex numbers of length 1, each a quarter turn further round. Their angles, in degrees, are 0 -90 180 90. A metro re-sends the pulse twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.real, np.imag and np.angle split a complex array; abs gives each number's length (its magnitude). np.fft.fft and np.linalg.eig make complex arrays. atan2 and hypot are the same pair of ideas for a point given as x and y.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the angle of each complex number: the direction from the origin to the point a + b j, measured counter-clockwise from the positive real axis. It is the phase of a frequency in a spectrum - where in its cycle that wave starts.\n\nTogether with abs (the length, the magnitude) it is the polar form of a complex number, as atan2 and hypot are for a point (x, y). Real numbers have angle 0, or 180 degrees (pi) when negative.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.angle\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA number, list or array, complex or not. Every input is converted and sent.\n\ndegrees:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default) gives radians, -pi to pi, as NumPy does;\non gives degrees, -180 to 180. The demo has it on.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nangle:\nThe angle of each number, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe spectrum of 0 1 0 0 - a single pulse one step in - is 1, -1j, -1 and 1j: four complex numbers of length 1, each a quarter turn further round. Their angles, in degrees, are 0 -90 180 90. A metro re-sends the pulse twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.real, np.imag and np.angle split a complex array; abs gives each number's length (its magnitude). np.fft.fft and np.linalg.eig make complex arrays. atan2 and hypot are the same pair of ideas for a point given as x and y."
        },
